index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..07afef36676c1bd52b634a811f01a0b17b2f53e1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/openspec/changes/v2-cycle-03-product-span-retrieval/design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+## Context
+
+See `proposal.md` for motivation and `specs/mixed-line-product-retrieval/spec.md` for the behavioral contract. The current hybrid path accepts the normalized raw query and one canonical alternate, with the raw query retained for reranking. Existing clause extraction removes list and quantity framing but does not isolate a product phrase from work-action wording.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Isolate the upstream question of whether surrounding work language obscures an explicitly named product.
+- Keep this query-time experiment deterministic, inspectable, bounded to one alternate, and fail-open.
+- Reuse the current alternate-query retrieval path and candidate fusion semantics whenever possible.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- No `NOT_FOUND` routing, service classification outcome, candidate gating, or final-selection change.
+- No product inference from a work verb, foreign model code, or catalog absence.
+- No additional semantic aliases, provider calls, index/document changes, or index rebuild.
+
+## Decisions
+
+### Recognize only an explicit action-plus-product clause
+
+Add a small, pure extraction step after source normalization. It is eligible only when a tightly allowlisted work-action phrase and a current supported product anchor occur within the same clause, with the action preceding the product anchor. The derived text begins at the product anchor and ends at the existing safe clause/quantity boundary; existing canonical transformations can then normalize only the retained product text.
+
+This avoids an action-word router: research shows some work lines contain a viable product, while service-only lines must not be silently treated as absence. It also avoids unbounded noun-phrase extraction or an LLM parser, both of which can discard constraints or invent specifications.
+
+### Preserve a single additive alternate
+
+The canonicalization result continues to expose at most one optional retrieval alternate. For a qualifying mixed clause, the product-bearing canonical text is that alternate; the raw text is always the primary query. The implementation must not issue both a generic canonical alternate and a product-span alternate in this experiment, which would confound the mechanism and expand the candidate pool beyond the fixed two-query path.
+
+This reuses the established hybrid-retriever API, per-modality deduplication, configured limits, and alternate-call failure handling. Extending fusion with a third variant was rejected because variant-aware candidate composition has already regressed safety and would make attribution ambiguous.
+
+### Keep selection boundaries unchanged
+
+`NomenclatureMatcher` passes the source query as today and uses the alternate only for hybrid candidate generation. Reranking, `MatchResult.query`, cache keys, serialized output, and no-reranker modes retain their current source-query behavior.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Action cue accidentally binds to another clause] → Require a same-clause, action-before-anchor relationship and decline uncertain cases.
+- [Span removes a necessary constraint] → Start at the explicit product anchor, end only at established safe boundaries, and test DN/PN, connection, material, and control retention.
+- [Alternate increases unsafe candidate availability] → Retain raw retrieval/reranking and the fixed candidate cap; outer public and hidden gates decide promotion.
+- [No measurable uplift because raw retrieval already succeeds] → The result is still useful falsification of line-item granularity as the bottleneck, with no persistent-data migration cost.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+1. Add the pure, narrowly allowlisted mixed-clause extraction and focused unit tests.
+2. Select its derived text as the existing optional canonical alternate only for eligible lines; leave all ineligible lines unchanged.
+3. Add matcher and mocked hybrid-retrieval regression tests for source-query preservation, one-alternate behavior, and alternate failure.
+4. Run focused tests and `python -m pytest -q`; the outer supervisor performs public/hidden evaluation and promotion.
+5. Roll back by removing the mixed-clause branch; no index, schema, or data migration is required.
